    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="0ca94573-8672-4372-9074-da09fea51f72">
      <data key="d3">The presence of a surrender charge discourages early withdrawal behavior; however, partial withdrawals remain permissible even under such conditions, provided they comply with specified limits and do not jeopardize policy status.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="78ae0a1e-3056-4039-bbc1-344b5b6b24a5">
      <data key="d3">Surrender charges limit early policy termination by imposing fees, which indirectly influence how much can be withdrawn without jeopardizing the policy's survival; thus, these charges reinforce the need to maintain sufficient account value after partial withdrawals.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="41c5d4e5-1a4c-49c6-93b9-86a0c912c977">
      <data key="d3">The surrender charge described in fact_sentence_1 complements the withdrawal fee in fact_sentence_2 by reinforcing financial disincentives against accessing policy value early; together, they form part of a broader penalty framework affecting policyholder behavior during the first decade.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="50663f78-143a-4016-b611-650692d96c08">
      <data key="d3">A surrender charge may increase if partial withdrawals reduce the account value significantly before surrender, potentially lowering the net payout. This interaction highlights how ongoing withdrawals impact final surrender outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="63a11648-dce1-4eab-baac-b85dc94452ef">
      <data key="d3">Surrender charges penalize early policy cancellation, potentially influencing decisions about taking loans; high surrender fees may deter borrowers from accessing funds via loans due to compounding costs.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences cost</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="6af7d75d-09de-48d0-a36c-4995b7e25cb6">
      <data key="d3">A surrender charge reduces the amount received upon terminating the policy, potentially limiting the effective use of accumulated account value, which could otherwise support large policy loans.</data>
      <data key="d4">independently relevant</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="ce5368c1-45d3-4d18-a2cd-ed25bfce9ea9">
      <data key="d3">The surrender charge reduces the available cash value upon policy cancellation, potentially affecting how much remains after subtracting outstanding loans. This interaction highlights cumulative financial reductions impacting final settlement.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="e467f66f-8ebe-41fd-8e9c-a879c062996c">
      <data key="d3">The Surrender Charge mentioned in fact_sentence_1 is a key component in calculating the Cash Surrender Value outlined in fact_sentence_2, meaning the fee reduces the amount an insured receives upon policy termination.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="733fa6c6-1173-4248-94bb-47741de40661">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes the general principle that surrender charges apply within the first 15 years; fact_sentence_2 provides specific confirmation that the charge in year one is 8%, reinforcing and concretizing the earlier statement.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="ca4ca85e-d0c7-4f15-93d4-03a1cb8f9b19">
      <data key="d3">The Surrender Charge defined in fact_sentence_1 is a key component used in calculating the Cash Surrender Value outlined in fact_sentence_2, making it an integral factor in determining the final payout upon policy termination.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="be5a6946-58ba-4487-a91b-97d9949ba78f">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 provides the general rule about surrender charges being percentage-based within 15 years, while fact_sentence_2 offers a concrete example by specifying a 5% charge in Year 5, illustrating the application of the general rule.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="d2bec389-f814-4532-8f19-68d9fc0c9d10">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes that surrender charges are applicable within the first 15 years, while fact_sentence_2 provides specific data by stating the Year 1 charge is 8% of the Specified Amount, making it a precise example of the general rule.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="afcce2ca-db53-4aa4-8a56-3863abb45a24">
      <data key="d3">The surrender charge exists precisely because surrendering the policy terminates coverage, making the fee contingent on the cessation of benefits described in fact_sentence_2.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="0155c9de-0b82-477a-9ec7-27cf965dc575">
      <data key="d3">The existence of a surrender charge depends on the policy being surrendered, which directly results in its termination. This creates a conditional dependency where the fee is triggered by the end-of-policy action described in fact_sentence_2.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="ddfa898f-d3e4-4e89-9fe3-ece66a9197ed">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 introduces the concept of a declining percentage-based surrender charge over 15 years, while fact_sentence_2 exemplifies this by specifying a 2% charge in Year 10, illustrating the application of the general rule.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="f6b5e972-2fde-453a-98fa-9ea8711ed5fa">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes that surrender charges apply before year 15, while fact_sentence_2 clarifies that such charges cease entirely beginning in year 15, forming a complete timeline of surrender fee applicability.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="c17b6902-b6ee-401e-82aa-c2427e58266a">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 explains a penalty for terminating the policy early, while fact_sentence_2 specifies reduced payouts in case of suicide within two years. Together, they highlight scenarios where full account value may not be paid out due to policy-specific restrictions.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="70a90dda-a504-430c-b48f-97a62091716e">
      <data key="d3">The existence of a 61-day grace period ensures that policies remain active even if account value cannot cover monthly deductions, potentially delaying any surrender until after the grace period expires, thereby affecting whether surrender charges apply.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="31c00839-d964-44d5-8027-45be3fef034a">
      <data key="d3">Surrender Charge directly affects the amount recoverable upon policy termination, influencing whether an excess account value remains after deducting these fees, which aligns with the automatic payout condition described in fact_sentence_2.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8" target="d24747c1-d663-4439-9454-0b5bd8f1185c">
      <data key="d3">The surrender charge applies only if the policy remains active beyond the grace period; otherwise, it lapses before surrender occurs. Therefore, the risk of paying a surrender charge depends on successfully maintaining the policy past the 61-day window.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="a1716a03-8fc0-4f38-af64-fe640c86dbd5" target="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8">
      <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
      <data key="d4">subject to surrender charge</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6a70e3fc-41dc-4f6e-9f6e-0986bee4cc7e" target="d59f0cd9-1a58-404b-85a4-9cde309d52b8">
      <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
      <data key="d4">is a fee applied when policy is surrendered within first 15 years</data>
    </edge>
